    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="aa7737fa-2c32-4ee2-8244-f4f6917dedc8">
      <data key="d3">The concept of Paid-Up Insurance—a form of permanent coverage requiring no further payments—is made feasible within the framework of a participating whole life policy, such as the one issued by Guardian Family Protection Corp.</data>
      <data key="d4">is feasible within</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="82aeed9b-92d1-465a-b1fa-fc66540d9d53">
      <data key="d3">The concept of Paid-Up Insurance applies specifically to whole life policies like the one described here, offering a permanent protection alternative after ceasing premium payments.</data>
      <data key="d4">applies to</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="2798b517-d05d-4a9e-a451-d86e9388a002">
      <data key="d3">Paid-Up Insurance provides a permanent death benefit without requiring future premiums, offering a lower payout than the original $250,000 face amount when converted from the base policy.</data>
      <data key="d4">explains</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="e1c25cef-f1a5-45b6-bce9-aecb6d7ba9a3">
      <data key="d3">The availability of Paid-Up Insurance provides a long-term alternative to ongoing premium payments, making the initial $3,240 annual premium part of a broader strategy for sustained coverage.</data>
      <data key="d4">part of strategy</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="93e5c855-70f6-47b8-99fd-a2bc45122e25">
      <data key="d3">The option to convert to Paid-Up Insurance implies that prior premium payments were made; since premiums here are specified as annual, they provide the foundation necessary for such conversions.</data>
      <data key="d4">required for</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="874ec04d-ba44-4087-bca5-6ad2e68a63c7">
      <data key="d3">The availability of Paid-Up Insurance provides a mechanism by which the requirement to pay premiums indefinitely—until age 100—can be terminated early through conversion, offering financial relief without losing coverage entirely.</data>
      <data key="d4">enables</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="c480fe05-ce9f-4a3f-850c-78f45358c91e">
      <data key="d3">Paid-Up Insurance relies on having sufficient Cash Surrender Value built up over time; it cannot exist independently unless such funds have been accrued through premium payments and dividends.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="07e5a874-62e0-4196-a2d8-9165fd8a7041">
      <data key="d3">Conversion to paid-up insurance eliminates future premiums but relies on accumulated cash value—such as what remains after subtracting loans and surrender charges from total cash value—to support the new benefit level.</data>
      <data key="d4">derives from</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="44d166e8-5546-4afd-be9d-b8fb4e61a5df">
      <data key="d3">Paid-Up Insurance provides permanent coverage without ongoing premiums, leveraging existing cash value. This contrasts with a Policy Loan, which draws on the same cash value but creates debt against it.</data>
      <data key="d4">modifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="7f9e8133-9ea9-4e40-b2c8-cccc14df47a1">
      <data key="d3">The availability of paid-up insurance as a conversion option relies on sufficient accumulated cash value, which can be enhanced over time through dividends derived from the company’s financial performance.</data>
      <data key="d4">facilitated by</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="4db504ec-839d-45cc-a54e-508072f29052">
      <data key="d3">The guaranteed cash value of $28,450 by Year 10 demonstrates the policy’s ability to accumulate funds that could support future conversion to paid-up insurance, aligning with the feature described in fact_sentence_1.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="982dcffc-8963-4a09-a00a-3a7afc345757">
      <data key="d3">Paid-up insurance depends on sufficient cash surrender value to maintain coverage without ongoing premiums, making the availability of loans based on that value relevant to its implementation.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="62ce7dc7-8d45-41ca-8622-42a08799f32e">
      <data key="d3">The concept of paid-up insurance depends on accumulated cash value, which becomes available for borrowing after one year, enabling policyholders to maintain benefits without ongoing payments.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="3aa4a1e9-a805-4aee-a558-83457fd0e547">
      <data key="d3">Converting to paid-up insurance eliminates future premiums but may require assessing ongoing loan obligations, such as those accruing at 5% annually, which could diminish the effective death benefit.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="e4e45c14-e67b-4e30-b13b-2739562070cd">
      <data key="d3">Paid-up insurance reduces the death benefit permanently, and any existing loans against the policy further decrease the final payout by subtracting accumulated debt and interest.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="6025efda-5892-4057-8c1a-24a08309d3ef">
      <data key="d3">Converting to paid-up insurance does not eliminate prior loans; thus, outstanding debt reduces the adjusted cash surrender value and death benefit despite the cessation of premiums.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.92</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="8a5bb177-c6ec-43a8-ab9b-8642558f8120">
      <data key="d3">The guaranteed cash value reaching $71,200 by Year 20 provides sufficient financial backing to support the transition into paid-up insurance, where future premiums cease while maintaining some level of coverage.</data>
      <data key="d4">enables</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="528ac7df-43ef-4675-962b-37d25d763e12">
      <data key="d3">The concept of paid-up insurance depends on sufficient cash value, which can be eroded by accumulating loan interest. If borrowers take loans against their policy’s cash value, compounding interest increases liabilities, potentially reducing the funds available to support paid-up insurance.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="4efbedc6-1cfd-4358-ac83-80bd56506e46">
      <data key="d3">The concept of paid-up insurance from fact_sentence_1 directly informs the claim settlement process detailed in fact_sentence_2, particularly regarding how reduced benefits apply when premiums cease and claims are settled.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="5ea73efa-2572-4665-a567-0bc08cfeff08">
      <data key="d3">The existence of paid-up insurance provides a viable settlement path that triggers the claim process described in fact_sentence_2, ensuring beneficiaries receive benefits promptly once eligible.</data>
      <data key="d4">enables processing</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.92</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="9054f79e-c024-42c1-ab31-f6a85012ac7b">
      <data key="d3">Paid-up insurance provides continued coverage without premiums, while cash surrender offers immediate liquidity instead of ongoing protection. Both are non-forfeiture options available after three years of premium payments.</data>
      <data key="d4">complementary options</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="747cc78d-f00a-40a8-979b-9890c223d923">
      <data key="d3">Paid-up insurance offers continued coverage without premiums, while cash surrender terminates the policy for immediate cash value. They represent competing options available when premiums end.</data>
      <data key="d4">alternative to</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="b13a632f-d54c-467f-9a7f-83f5e5c712e6">
      <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 defines Reduced Paid-Up Insurance as a permanent benefit with no future premiums, which directly corresponds to the non-forfeiture option described in fact_sentence_2 when premiums are halted after three years.</data>
      <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="0c1d3d36-ecf7-4afe-973c-5c905fcb9ec8">
      <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 explains that paid-up insurance offers a lower death benefit without future premiums, which matches exactly what fact_sentence_2 describes about reduced paid-up insurance as a continuation of coverage under similar conditions.</data>
      <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.98</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="63ff27ef-e3e0-4f42-bbe9-ee3129a67647">
      <data key="d3">Paid-up insurance provides permanent coverage without future premiums, while extended term insurance offers temporary coverage using accumulated cash value. Both serve as alternatives when premiums end, making them complementary risk mitigation tools.</data>
      <data key="d4">complements</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="10bf0615-4e53-4ca9-9f8f-24f248922077">
      <data key="d3">Paid-up insurance reduces the death benefit permanently but eliminates premiums, while extended term insurance preserves the original face amount temporarily via cash value use—both serve as post-premium cessation options.</data>
      <data key="d4">complementary options</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="d9857520-5a18-4a32-8752-3f61f66f696a">
      <data key="d3">Paid-up insurance provides a permanent, reduced death benefit without ongoing premiums, similar to extended term insurance, which also offers continued coverage without additional payments after premium stops.</data>
      <data key="d4">complements</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="f96958f0-bbcb-476e-be0c-004fac2bd620">
      <data key="d3">Paid-up insurance offers a permanent death benefit without ongoing payments, serving as a viable option when premiums cease, while extended term provides temporary coverage instead. They represent different outcomes available upon lapse.</data>
      <data key="d4">alternative to</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="b3ac5002-b47d-45fd-8c6f-813289686f71">
      <data key="d3">The concept of paid-up insurance provides a permanent alternative when premiums cease, whereas reinstatement offers a path to restore coverage after lapse, requiring new underwriting approval.</data>
      <data key="d4">complements</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="777d5357-97c8-4051-86e0-1ca68438a2f4">
      <data key="d3">Paid-up insurance offers a permanent death benefit without ongoing premiums, serving as a viable alternative when reinstatement is not chosen after lapse. This complements the reinstatement condition described in fact_sentence_2, where unpaid premiums must be settled with interest.</data>
      <data key="d4">alternative to</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="1aca0368-1bec-44f3-997c-933cfa6b6cff">
      <data key="d3">Paid-up insurance eliminates future premiums but does not address loan repayments needed for reinstatement. This creates a conditional dependency: once converted to paid-up, reinstatement via loan repayment becomes irrelevant since no new premiums apply.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="fdebe47d-6d0f-4dce-a65b-cbc219fc28d4">
      <data key="d3">The existence of paid-up insurance depends on adjustments due to misstated ages, which can reduce the final death benefit accordingly.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea" target="0f2cfa5e-fb62-4758-8fe0-1102a3d97086">
      <data key="d3">The concept of paid-up insurance provides a permanent coverage alternative upon ceasing premium payments, which complements the policy's flexible exit options including the 30-day free look cancellation right.</data>
      <data key="d4">complements</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
