    </edge>
    <edge source="13b1ef68-ef2d-411e-9021-06ccea514adb" target="84a7b196-2336-4dd5-8ec6-5834754c1add">
      <data key="d3">Because dividends are not guaranteed, they may not materialize, limiting their ability to boost the cash value beyond the guaranteed projections.</data>
      <data key="d4">limits possibility</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="13b1ef68-ef2d-411e-9021-06ccea514adb" target="760d34d4-eb2d-414c-baa9-b87b4f1a8e93">
      <data key="d3">Since dividends are not guaranteed, the participatory feature mentioned in sentence 1 comes with inherent variability, making actual payouts uncertain despite the policy's classification as participating.</data>
      <data key="d4">explains</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="13b1ef68-ef2d-411e-9021-06ccea514adb" target="f7f0f5d5-2d0e-4014-aa5a-76bbb9faf578">
      <data key="d3">Because dividends are not guaranteed, even if the insurer performs well in terms of mortality, policyholders cannot rely on receiving them annually.</data>
      <data key="d4">not guaranteed</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="13b1ef68-ef2d-411e-9021-06ccea514adb" target="ea3ff14c-b0b2-41ce-b2c5-75781d17406d">
      <data key="d3">Because dividends are not guaranteed, even if the insurer performs well, policyholders cannot expect them, making future dividend amounts speculative despite favorable investment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d4">not guaranteed</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="13b1ef68-ef2d-411e-9021-06ccea514adb" target="23c1dfbe-c779-47ed-b0fc-14e97169d328">
      <data key="d3">Because dividends are not guaranteed, even if the insurer performs well financially, policyholders cannot rely on receiving them annually.</data>
      <data key="d4">not guaranteed</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="13b1ef68-ef2d-411e-9021-06ccea514adb" target="39a1ab26-48c9-45c8-bbe9-0682552a3e65">
      <data key="d3">Since dividends are not guaranteed, their actual receipt is uncertain. However, when received, one possible allocation method is using them to buy additional insurance through paid-up additions.</data>
      <data key="d4">conditional dependency</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="13b1ef68-ef2d-411e-9021-06ccea514adb" target="20487c89-e0e7-4387-abc3-4e1f3259ca1f">
      <data key="d3">Since dividends are not guaranteed, the possibility of using them via options like "Accumulate at Interest" remains uncertain. This creates a contingent relationship where the second fact relies on the existence of dividends mentioned in the first.</data>
      <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="13b1ef68-ef2d-411e-9021-06ccea514adb" target="e3e246c1-096d-44df-8e6e-335cc7374485">
      <data key="d3">Since dividends are not guaranteed, the availability of funds to allocate into paid-up additions cannot be assured. Therefore, the selection of "Paid-Up Additions" as the dividend option is dependent on uncertain future dividend payouts.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="13b1ef68-ef2d-411e-9021-06ccea514adb" target="982dcffc-8963-4a09-a00a-3a7afc345757">
      <data key="d3">Non-guaranteed dividends contribute to increasing the cash surrender value over time, which in turn affects the maximum loan amount available under the policy.</data>
      <data key="d4">influences</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="13b1ef68-ef2d-411e-9021-06ccea514adb" target="9385aac2-9570-4e2e-8922-87d7cad0451d">
      <data key="d3">Since dividends are not guaranteed, their actual amounts may fall short of projections, potentially limiting cash value growth. This reduced growth raises the likelihood that accumulated debt could eventually exceed the available cash value, leading to policy termination.</data>
      <data key="d4">increases risk of</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="13b1ef68-ef2d-411e-9021-06ccea514adb" target="0e45bbad-ae20-4562-bed9-67e55a13cadc">
      <data key="d3">The non-guaranteed dividends contribute to the accumulation of paid-up additions, which increase the total death benefit. This connection shows how dividend allocation indirectly supports higher payouts under the policy's terms.</data>
      <data key="d4">influences</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="13b1ef68-ef2d-411e-9021-06ccea514adb" target="6025efda-5892-4057-8c1a-24a08309d3ef">
      <data key="d3">Non-guaranteed dividends contribute to increasing the cash value over time, indirectly affecting the pool available before deductions like loan repayments. However, even without dividends, outstanding loans still reduce the cash surrender value regardless of dividend performance.</data>
      <data key="d4">indirectly affects</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
