    </edge>
    <edge source="4efbedc6-1cfd-4358-ac83-80bd56506e46" target="0c1d3d36-ecf7-4afe-973c-5c905fcb9ec8">
      <data key="d3">The payment terms described in fact_sentence_1 set the stage for understanding post-death payouts, while fact_sentence_2 outlines a specific option available when premiums end—Reduced Paid-Up Insurance—which ensures continued protection despite no further contributions.</data>
      <data key="d4">exemplifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4efbedc6-1cfd-4358-ac83-80bd56506e46" target="fdebe47d-6d0f-4dce-a65b-cbc219fc28d4">
      <data key="d3">The payout under fact_sentence_1 depends on the corrected face amount when the insured’s age was misrepresented, meaning that the initial face amount may need adjustment before applying the formula.</data>
      <data key="d4">modifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4efbedc6-1cfd-4358-ac83-80bd56506e46" target="4871bb62-321f-47dd-8890-1d5bf5de7bcf">
      <data key="d3">The standard death benefit calculation applies unless the death results from suicide within two years, in which case the payout is limited to premiums paid.</data>
      <data key="d4">modifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4efbedc6-1cfd-4358-ac83-80bd56506e46" target="d9857520-5a18-4a32-8752-3f61f66f696a">
      <data key="d3">After proving death, the insurer pays the net death benefit, which may include extended term insurance as an option. Under extended term insurance, no future premiums are due, making it a viable choice without ongoing financial obligation.</data>
      <data key="d4">exemplifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4efbedc6-1cfd-4358-ac83-80bd56506e46" target="0e45bbad-ae20-4562-bed9-67e55a13cadc">
      <data key="d3">Fact sentence 2 reiterates the exact payout mechanism described in fact sentence 1, reinforcing its validity through consistent terminology and structure despite differing contextual framing.</data>
      <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">1.0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4efbedc6-1cfd-4358-ac83-80bd56506e46" target="07e5a874-62e0-4196-a2d8-9165fd8a7041">
      <data key="d3">Both statements address reductions due to outstanding loans—one specifying it affects cash surrender value upon termination, the other detailing its role in reducing the death benefit payment.</data>
      <data key="d4">related to</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4efbedc6-1cfd-4358-ac83-80bd56506e46" target="310584c0-76a7-4455-8d4e-410439b625ea">
      <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 elaborates on the payment terms upon death, confirming that paid-up insurance outcomes—including reduced payouts—are part of the overall benefit distribution framework discussed in fact_sentence_1.</data>
      <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
